+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Keel's proportionality table puts every "config value" on the lightest row (nothing beyond the
+task record / summary). That is right for inert values, but wrong for a one-line change to a
+Runtime timeout, a retry limit, or a permission setting: such a change alters runtime behaviour
+or security with the same diff size as a comment edit, yet would go to review with no card and
+no behavioural evidence. The tables already say "Risk is read from what the change touches,
+not from how big it is"; the row that admits all config values contradicts it.
+
+## What Changes
+
+Docs-only change; no checker, no new rule in `extensions/builtin/graphhelm-development-contracts/policies/keel.yaml`, no code:
+
+- `AGENTS.md` (Keel section) and `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (§2): limit the light row to
+  **inert** config values — a value whose changed setting cannot alter runtime behaviour or
+  security.
+- Same two tables: add **runtime- or security-affecting config** to the expanded-route row's
+  trigger list (`persistence, permissions, compatibility, security, external effects`).
+- Same two documents, next to each table: state explicitly that a runtime- or
+  security-affecting config value needs the full card **and behavioural evidence** that the
+  observed behaviour changes — parsing or shape validation alone is not proof (the existing
+  Journey-Proven Development proof rule already says exactly this; the table statement points
+  at it instead of restating a second version).
+- Not touched: the deterministic half of Keel (`keel.yaml`, `core/policy`), the Keel spec and
+  paradigms paper, `plugins/graphhelm/README.md`, and the benchmark protocol. If the owner
+  later wants the classifier to enforce the tier split, that is a separate change with its own
+  pathogen and version bump.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `keel-card-proportionality`: how the delivery process routes a change into the tier of card
+  and evidence it requires, keyed to what the change touches — including the inert vs
+  runtime-/security-affecting config split.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+(None. No capabilities exist under `openspec/specs/` yet; this is the first spec in the
+inventory.)
+
+## Impact
+
+- Files: `AGENTS.md`, `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (two table rows each plus one adjacent
+  sentence each).
+- No code, tests, schemas, dependencies, or wire contracts change. The only consumers are
+  agents and humans reading the delivery rules at session start; the observable contract is
+  the wording itself, checked by rereading both tables after the edit.
+- Precedence note: these documents sit below `docs/DECISION_REGISTER.md` and accepted ADRs in
+  the conflict order. No register entry or ADR assigns config values a tier, so restricting the
+  light row introduces no contradiction with a higher-precedence source; the JPD proof rule in
+  the same files already requires behavioural evidence for runtime-affecting configuration.
