+# Spec Delta
+
+## Purpose
+
+Defines how the GraphHelm delivery process routes each kind of change into the tier of Keel card and proof it requires, so that ceremony tracks what the change can reach — including which config values may travel light and which need a full card plus behavioural evidence.
+
+## ADDED Requirements
+
+### Requirement: Light tier admits only inert config values
+The proportionality tables (`AGENTS.md` Keel section, `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` section 2) SHALL limit their lightest row to documentation edits, comments, inert config values, one-line fixes, and test-only fixes. A config value is inert when its changed setting cannot alter runtime behaviour or security; every other config value is runtime- or security-affecting.
+
+#### Scenario: Inert config value stays light
+- **WHEN** a change alters only a config value whose setting cannot change runtime behaviour or security (for example, a display label)
+- **THEN** the lightest row applies and nothing beyond the task record or summary is required in the pull request body
+
+#### Scenario: Behaviour-changing config value leaves the light row
+- **WHEN** a change alters a Runtime timeout, a retry limit, or a permission setting
+- **THEN** the lightest row does not apply and the change takes the tier the tables assign to runtime- or security-affecting config
+
+### Requirement: Runtime- or security-affecting config takes the full card and behavioural evidence
+A change to runtime- or security-affecting config SHALL be listed among the expanded-route triggers in both tables and SHALL carry the full card with the JPD flow, and its pull request SHALL name behavioural evidence that the setting changes observed behaviour; parsing or shape validation alone is not proof.
+
+#### Scenario: Timeout change shows behavioural evidence
+- **WHEN** a pull request changes a Runtime timeout and its review checks the card
+- **THEN** the pull request body carries the full card and names a test or command whose outcome depends on the timeout actually firing, not merely on the config parsing
+
+#### Scenario: Parse-only validation is not proof
+- **WHEN** the only evidence attached to a runtime- or security-affecting config change is that the value parses or validates against its schema
+- **THEN** the change lacks the behavioural evidence the tables require and the review records it as missing
+
+### Requirement: The two published tables stay mutually consistent
+`AGENTS.md` (Keel section) and `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (section 2) publish the same routing in two wordings; a reclassification of any row in one table SHALL be applied to the matching row of the other table in the same change.
+
+#### Scenario: Row reclassified in one file
+- **WHEN** a later change moves a kind of change between rows in either table
+- **THEN** the same change updates the matching row in the other table and the pull request body names both files
+
+#### Scenario: Reviewer compares the two tables
+- **WHEN** a reviewer compares the row sets of the two tables
+- **THEN** every kind of change named in either table sits in the same tier in both, with matching asks: task record or summary, three-line card, full card, or full card with the JPD flow
